          %p
          #Attribution.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (attribution)
              %br/
              For the case of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphisms"} diffeomorphisms
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Embedding"}
              was proven by
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Pursell52"} Pursell (1952)
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Shanks51"} Shanks (1951)
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Grabowski78"} Grabowski (1978)
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#MarsdenRatiuAbraham02"} Marsden, Ratiu &amp; Abraham (2002)
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Grabowski05"} Grabowski (2005)
            %p
              For the case that the domain is a point the statement is contained in the dissertation of
              %a{:href => "#Pursell"} Pursell
              (Section 8), see Lemma
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MilnorExercise"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exercise"} exercise
              (without reference to Pursell) in
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#MilnorStasheff74"} Milnor &amp; Stasheff (1974) §1, Problem 1-C (p. 11)
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#KolarSlovakMichor93"} Kolář, Slovák &amp; Michor (1993) 35.8-9
            %p
              The general statement of Theorem
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Embedding"}
              appears as
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#KolarSlovakMichor93"} Kolář, Slovák &amp; Michor (1993) 35.10
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The statement of theorem
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Embedding"}
              serves as the stepping-stone for generalizations of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
              such as to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
              (such as
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+differentials"} Kähler differentials
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
              structure, one needs to remember the
              = succeed "-structure" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+algebra"} smooth algebra
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              If one drops the second countability assumption on manifolds then theorem
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Embedding"}
              may break. For instance allowing
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} uncountably
              many connected components, then there are counterexamples (
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/a/91445"}> MO discussion
              ).
          %h2#related_theorems Related theorems
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel%27s+theorem"} Borel's theorem
            %li
              %p
